{
  "teamId": 146,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Miami Marlins were a team that never found its footing, opening the year 0-4 before April spiraled into a brutal 7-20 record that buried any early optimism. A pitching staff that would finish with a 4.73 ERA and an offense that could manage only a .678 OPS simply could not compete night after night, and the season's highest-leverage moments were dominated by opposing hitters making Marlins relievers pay dearly — most memorably Tanner Scott surrendering a three-run Marcell Ozuna blast in April and a J.D. Martinez homer in June that extinguished rally after rally.\n\nMay offered the one true exhale of the season, a 14-13 month that felt like a revelation by Miami's standards. Bryan De La Cruz emerged as the team's offensive heartbeat, and supporting cast members like Otto Lopez, Josh Bell, and Jesús Sánchez delivered timely hits that briefly made the Marlins look like a functional big-league club. It was fleeting proof that the roster had pieces worth watching, even if the whole was far less than the sum of its parts.\n\nJune snapped the illusion almost immediately, dropping Miami to 9-17, and the summer months were an exercise in attrition. The trade deadline came and went without meaningful reinforcement, key relievers continued to falter in high-leverage spots, and a 204-run run differential told the brutal mathematical story — opponents simply outclassed them at every turn. The bullpen was a recurring wound, and errors and baserunning gaffes compounded losses that might otherwise have been recoverable.\n\nSeptember brought a modest 12-14 finish, not enough to salvage dignity in the standings but enough to hint at some young players worth monitoring in 2025. The Marlins closed at 62-100, a franchise low-water mark that reflected a roster in genuine transition. For a team that had made the playoffs just two years prior, the 2024 season was a stark reminder of how quickly a thin margin for error becomes an unforgiving spiral when the pitching collapses and the offense goes quiet.",
  "chapters": [
